// devkit owns this file. It is the CANONICAL alex-projects.code-workspace.
//
// The live workspace file sits outside every repo, so for years it was the source of
// truth and this copy was a mirror taken from it. That inverted the only property
// worth having: the file every agent writes to had no branch, no history and no
// review, so two sessions editing it raced with last-writer-wins, and a single
// session's in-flight edit was globally live before its PR was even opened. It is
// how alex-projects.code-workspace came to be running an UNMERGED branch's task
// block (PR #177, 2026-08-21) for every window on the machine.
//
// So the direction is now canonical -> live:
//
//     python scripts/devkit_project.py --check-workspace    // do they agree?
//     python scripts/devkit_project.py --render-workspace   // this file -> the live one
//     python scripts/devkit_project.py --adopt-workspace    // the live one -> this file
//
// Edit THIS file, on a task branch, and let `--render-workspace` publish it once the
// PR merges. Two agents then conflict in git, where a conflict is visible and
// resolvable, instead of in a file with no history. `--adopt-workspace` remains for
// the other direction — a hand edit in VS Code, or a workspace setting the editor
// wrote itself — and rendering refuses rather than overwriting one.
//
// Comparison is SEMANTIC (parsed), not byte-for-byte: VS Code reformats this file
// when it writes a setting through its own UI, and flagging that would train
// everyone to ignore the check.
{
	// The one workspace. Every checkout lives here, and `sweep.py` reads this
	// `folders` list as the project registry — a project missing from it is invisible
	// to every sweep, so nothing reports its stranded work: the session-start line
	// (`workspace-status.py`) and reconcile's checkout pass both read this list, and
	// there is no longer a task to run by hand instead. `new-project.py`
	// registers new projects here rather than writing a workspace file of its own.
	//
	// A *reference* checkout — one registered here to be read but never shipped from —
	// goes in `sweep.DEFAULT_EXCLUDE` and `devkit_project.NOT_PROJECTS` as well. Both
	// sets are empty today: every folder below is a real project.
	"folders": [
		{
			"path": "carameli"
		},
		{
			"path": "ibkr_trader"
		},
		{
			"path": "devkit"
		},
		{
			"path": "data-lake"
		},
		{
			"path": "sports_betting"
		},
		{
			"path": "roguelike"
		}
	],
	"extensions": {
		"recommendations": [
			"rioj7.command-variable"
		]
	},
	// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
	// EVERY task lives here. There are no project-level `.vscode/tasks.json` files left
	// in this workspace, and re-growing one is now a test failure in the repo that does
	// it rather than a matter of taste.
	//
	// The reason is arithmetic, not purity. This is a MULTI-ROOT workspace and every
	// repo is checked out twice — carameli/carameli-b, ibkr_trader/ibkr_trader-b. A task
	// defined inside a repo is therefore rendered once per FOLDER: two quick-pick entries
	// carrying the same label, nothing to say which checkout each one would run in, and
	// two copies that disagree the moment the worktrees sit on different branches. That
	// last part was not hypothetical — when these were deleted, carameli's two copies
	// had drifted to eight tasks versus two, and ibkr_trader's to five versus eleven,
	// with the SAME label ("Stop: Docker Stack") running different commands.
	//
	// So a project-specific task is not laundered into a generic one on the way up. It
	// keeps its project-specific script and gains a picker over the two checkouts that
	// can actually run it — which is all the `--project` argument ever was.
	// `devkit_project.py`'s `Action.projects` is the enforcement: `test-target`, `e2e`
	// and `ngrok` are defined for the carameli pair only, the five IBKR actions for the
	// ibkr_trader pair only, and the dispatcher refuses any other checkout BY NAME
	// instead of falling through to "does not implement this action", which reads like
	// an invitation to go implement it.
	//
	// Not everything that was in those files came up. The three-task frontend-preview
	// composite stayed deleted because it was broken, not merely duplicated: its Simple
	// Browser opened localhost:5174, while ports.toml allocates 5173 to carameli and
	// 5175 to carameli-b, so it had never once pointed at a running server in either
	// checkout. ibkr_trader's five hidden snapshot steps and carameli's stack start/stop
	// are here as one task each ("Snapshot: Run Monthly", "Docker: Start/Stop Stack").
	//
	// Every task routes through `devkit_project.py`, which resolves the picked name
	// against the `folders` list above and runs the action with cwd set to that
	// checkout. That replaced `${fileWorkspaceFolder}`, which needed an editor tab
	// open to anchor on and resolved to nothing when the picker was the only input.
	//
	// `${workspaceFolder:devkit}` (not `${workspaceFolder}`) is required: this is a
	// multi-root workspace, so the bare form is ambiguous and VS Code will not resolve
	// it. The named form is also why this file needs no absolute path.
	//
	// devkit owns and tests every script called here. If you are reading this in the
	// live `.code-workspace`, you are reading a RENDER: the copy with a branch and a
	// reviewer is `devkit/workspace.jsonc`, and `devkit_project.py --check-workspace`
	// is the gate between them. Edit the canonical one and
	// `--render-workspace`; `--adopt-workspace` goes the other way, for an edit VS
	// Code itself made. `.claude/rules/vscode-tasks.md` carries the why.
	//
	// Conventions. Label "Domain: Title Case Action". Every task carries a `detail` —
	// the second line of the quick-pick, and the only place a one-click action can state
	// its cost or blast radius. Every task carries an `icon`, and no two share the same
	// id+colour pair: with everything in one list, colour is what makes it scannable —
	// green quality, yellow lint/teardown, cyan sync, blue inspection, red destructive,
	// magenta ship/launch, and the BRIGHT variants for the project-scoped tasks, which
	// is how you can see at a glance that a task will ask you which checkout to use.
	"tasks":
	{
		"version": "2.0.0",
		"tasks": [
			// --- generic: pick a project ---------------------------------------
			{
				// Six tasks became this one. "Test: Run Suite" and its scope dropdown,
				// "Test: Harness Hook Tests — free", and carameli's "Run Carameli Target",
				// "Run Browser E2E" and "Run Local Integration E2E" all asked the same two
				// questions — which checkouts, and which tests — and answered the second
				// with a different single-pick each. Both are checkbox lists now and the
				// answers run as a cross-product: `TEST_KINDS` in devkit_project.py is the
				// menu, and a kind a checkout cannot run is skipped BY NAME rather than
				// dropped, because one menu is offered for every checkout.
				//
				// The sixth is the paid live-CLI smoke, which used to be a task of its own
				// so that a cost could not be reached by a keystroke from a free row. It is
				// two rows here now, one per CLI, and ticking both is what `both` was. The
				// trade that bought is worth stating where it is read: `testKinds` REMEMBERS
				// the ticked set per workspace, so a paid row ticked once is ticked on the
				// next click too, and this task can spend money without asking again. The
				// rows say PAID in their label, the runner prints what it is about to spend
				// before it launches anything, and neither of those is a confirmation.
				"label": "Test: Run Suite",
				"detail": "Tick one or more checkouts, then tick the kinds of test to run in them. Every pair runs sequentially and writes its own artifact under that checkout's logs/ — read the artifact, not the terminal. A kind only Carameli implements (its free targets, Playwright, the local integration suite) is skipped by name for the checkouts that cannot run it, so ticking widely is safe. The two rows marked PAID launch real authenticated agent CLI sessions and are devkit-only; the ticked set is REMEMBERED, so untick them again.",
				"type": "process",
				"command": "python",
				"args": [
					"${workspaceFolder:devkit}/scripts/devkit_project.py",
					"--project",
					"${input:project}",
					"tests",
					"${input:testKinds}"
				],
				"group": {
					"kind": "test",
					"isDefault": true
				},
				"icon": {
					"id": "beaker",
					"color": "terminal.ansiGreen"
				},
				"presentation": {
					"reveal": "always",
					"panel": "new",
					"showReuseMessage": false,
					"close": false
				},
				"problemMatcher": []
			},
			{
				// One task, two scopes. It was two tasks — "Lint: Everything" and
				// "Lint: Changed Files" — sitting adjacent in the picker under near-identical
				// labels, which is the shape "Test: Run Suite" has never had: there the scope
				// is asked as a picker, and the second question is asked only once the first
				// has been answered. `lintScope` is that dropdown, and `lint-changed` is gone
				// from the dispatcher with it.
				"label": "Lint: Run",
				"detail": "Lint each selected checkout, sequentially, via its scripts/lint-all.py — the whole repo, or only the working-tree diff (vs HEAD, plus untracked), as the scope dropdown asks. Rewrites the tree before it reports (ruff --fix, ruff format) and ships what it wrote. Findings go to that checkout's logs/lint-errors.log.",
				"type": "process",
				"command": "python",
				"args": [
					"${workspaceFolder:devkit}/scripts/devkit_project.py",
					"--project",
					"${input:project}",
					"lint",
					"${input:lintScope}"
				],
				"group": "build",
				"icon": {
					"id": "checklist",
					"color": "terminal.ansiYellow"
				},
				"presentation": {
					"reveal": "always",
					"panel": "new",
					"showReuseMessage": false,
					"close": false
				},
				"problemMatcher": []
			},
			{
				"label": "Agent: Sync Codex Context",
				"detail": "Regenerate Codex repository skills and hook configuration in each selected checkout, sequentially. Changes from a clean home branch are packaged as autofix-labelled PRs and auto-merged after the gate passes.",
				"type": "process",
				"command": "python",
				"args": [
					"${workspaceFolder:devkit}/scripts/devkit_project.py",
					"--project",
					"${input:project}",
					"sync-codex"
				],
				"icon": {
					"id": "sync",
					"color": "terminal.ansiCyan"
				},
				"presentation": {
					"reveal": "silent",
					"panel": "new",
					"showReuseMessage": false,
					"close": false
				},
				"problemMatcher": []
			},
			// --- the workspace file itself ------------------------------------
			// This file has no branch: one copy serves every VS Code window on the
			// machine, so an edit is globally live before anyone reviews it. devkit's
			// `workspace.jsonc` is the copy with a branch and a reviewer, and these
			// two are the only sanctioned way to move content between them. They are
			// NOT dispatches -- there is one workspace file, so there is nothing to
			// pick a checkout for, and the wrapping is written out here.
			//
			// There were three. "Workspace: Check Drift vs devkit's Copy" ran
			// `--check-workspace`, which is the read-only half of the publish below --
			// and the publish REFUSES an unadopted live edit and names every difference
			// while refusing, so the check answered a question the click you were going
			// to make answers anyway. The session-start line asks it every window as
			// well. Nothing routed through it that publishing does not.
			{
				"label": "Workspace: Publish devkit's Copy",
				"detail": "Renders devkit's workspace.jsonc over the live .code-workspace — the file every VS Code window on this machine reads. REFUSES rather than clobbering a live edit devkit never wrote; adopt that first. Publishes whatever branch the devkit checkout is on, so check it out before clicking.",
				"type": "process",
				"command": "python",
				"args": [
					"scripts/notify-wrap.py",
					"Workspace: Publish devkit's Copy",
					"--",
					"python",
					"scripts/log-wrap.py",
					"Workspace: Publish devkit's Copy",
					"--",
					"python",
					"scripts/devkit_project.py",
					"--render-workspace"
				],
				"options": {
					"cwd": "${workspaceFolder:devkit}"
				},
				"icon": {
					"id": "cloud-upload",
					"color": "terminal.ansiRed"
				},
				"presentation": {
					"reveal": "always",
					"panel": "new",
					"showReuseMessage": false,
					"close": false
				},
				"problemMatcher": []
			},
			{
				"label": "Workspace: Adopt Live Edits into devkit",
				"detail": "The other direction, for edits VS Code made itself — a workspace setting changed through its UI. Records the live file back into devkit's workspace.jsonc and leaves it UNCOMMITTED, so it reads as stranded work until you ship it on a task branch.",
				"type": "process",
				"command": "python",
				"args": [
					"scripts/notify-wrap.py",
					"Workspace: Adopt Live Edits into devkit",
					"--",
					"python",
					"scripts/log-wrap.py",
					"Workspace: Adopt Live Edits into devkit",
					"--",
					"python",
					"scripts/devkit_project.py",
					"--adopt-workspace"
				],
				"options": {
					"cwd": "${workspaceFolder:devkit}"
				},
				"icon": {
					"id": "save",
					"color": "terminal.ansiCyan"
				},
				"presentation": {
					"reveal": "always",
					"panel": "new",
					"showReuseMessage": false,
					"close": false
				},
				"problemMatcher": []
			},
			{
				// Read-only, and the fourth of the workspace-file tasks for the same
				// reason the other three exist: this file is the one place a reviewer
				// cannot see what it is reviewing. Forty tasks and forty pickers spread
				// over two thousand lines of JSONC, with `presentation`, `icon` and
				// `problemMatcher` between every pair of facts worth reading.
				"label": "Workspace: List Tasks as a Table",
				"detail": "Read-only. Prints every task in the workspace — what it runs, which ${input:...} pickers it asks first, and its detail — as a wrapped table, then the pickers themselves with every answer on offer and the tasks that ask for each. A picker no task uses shows as (unused). Also written to devkit/logs/workspace-tasks.txt, which is git-ignored: it is a generated view of a file already in the repo.",
				"type": "process",
				"command": "python",
				"args": [
					"scripts/log-wrap.py",
					"Workspace: List Tasks as a Table",
					"--",
					"python",
					"scripts/workspace-task-index.py",
					"${input:taskIndexSource}"
				],
				"options": {
					"cwd": "${workspaceFolder:devkit}"
				},
				"icon": {
					"id": "table",
					"color": "terminal.ansiBlue"
				},
				"presentation": {
					"reveal": "always",
					"panel": "new",
					"showReuseMessage": false,
					"close": false
				},
				"problemMatcher": []
			},
			{
				// The checkboxes are VS Code's, not the terminal's: `plugSelection` draws
				// every candidate as a `multiPick` quick-pick, pre-ticked from the registry,
				// and this task hands the answer back with `--ticked`. The pick IS the
				// confirmation — Escape cancels, every row's second line says what ticking
				// it costs, and a hazardous untick still refuses — which is what `--yes`
				// rests on and why no question is asked once the terminal opens.
				//
				// Still no `log-wrap.py`, and now for one reason rather than two: the script
				// writes `logs/plug-projects.log` itself, so the one artifact covers this
				// task and the bare CLI, whose checkbox loop log-wrap could not carry
				// anyway (it pipes stdout by line, so an unfinished prompt never appears).
				"label": "Workspace: Plug / Unplug Projects",
				"detail": "Checklist of every project — in the workspace registry, on disk, and on GitHub — ticked as the registry has them. Ticking an unplugged one clones its repo, or creates the repo when only the folder exists; unticking removes it from the registry and touches NOTHING on disk. Edits devkit's workspace.jsonc and republishes the live .code-workspace, so it refuses from a task branch or a box.",
				"type": "process",
				"command": "python",
				"args": [
					"scripts/notify-wrap.py",
					"Workspace: Plug / Unplug Projects",
					"--",
					"python",
					"scripts/plug-projects.py",
					"--ticked",
					"${input:plugSelection}",
					"--yes"
				],
				"options": {
					"cwd": "${workspaceFolder:devkit}"
				},
				"icon": {
					"id": "checklist",
					"color": "terminal.ansiBrightGreen"
				},
				"presentation": {
					"reveal": "always",
					"panel": "new",
					"showReuseMessage": false,
					"close": false
				},
				"problemMatcher": []
			},
			{
				// The one task that can reach a checkout with no harness, and the reason
				// it is not a devkit_project.py dispatch: that dispatcher resolves through
				// `known_projects`, which drops NOT_PROJECTS because every action it
				// runs needs a harness. Merging a trunk in needs git and nothing else,
				// so this script resolves against the raw registry — which is why its
				// picker can still list a reference checkout once one is registered.
				//
				// It MERGES, which is what long-lived work wants: the history is already
				// published, so it must not be rewritten. There used to be a sibling above
				// it, "Git: Sync Branch onto Origin Default (Keep Changes)", which rebased
				// the current branch and stashed around it -- the task-branch case. It is
				// gone because VS Code's own Source Control view already does exactly that,
				// per checkout, without a picker. This one stays for what that view does
				// not give: a conflict left in progress with every unmerged file named in
				// a log file, which is what makes the outcome handable to an agent.
				"label": "Git: Merge Origin Default into Current Branch",
				"detail": "Fetch, then MERGE origin's default branch — auto-detected per checkout, so a repo whose trunk is `develop` merges `develop` — into whatever branch the chosen checkout is on. Commits locally and NEVER pushes. On conflict the merge is LEFT IN PROGRESS and every unmerged file is named in devkit's logs/git-merge-origin-default-into-current-branch.log; hand that file to a coding agent.",
				"type": "process",
				"command": "python",
				"args": [
					"scripts/notify-wrap.py",
					"Git: Merge Origin Default into Current Branch",
					"--",
					"python",
					"scripts/log-wrap.py",
					"Git: Merge Origin Default into Current Branch",
					"--",
					"python",
					"scripts/git-merge-default.py",
					"--checkout",
					"${input:mergeCheckout}",
					"--base",
					"${input:mergeBase}"
				],
				"options": {
					"cwd": "${workspaceFolder:devkit}"
				},
				"icon": {
					"id": "git-merge",
					"color": "terminal.ansiBrightCyan"
				},
				"presentation": {
					"reveal": "always",
					"panel": "new",
					"showReuseMessage": false,
					"close": false
				},
				"problemMatcher": []
			},
			// --- generic: Docker ------------------------------------------------
			// Two kinds live here. The first two are STACK-scoped: they act on the
			// chosen project's own compose topology, which genuinely differs (carameli
			// waits on healthchecks, ibkr_trader scopes to its ibkr/app profiles), so
			// they delegate to that project's script and the shared thing is the CLI
			// contract. The rest are DAEMON-scoped — one Docker Desktop per machine —
			// and delegate only so a project can override the teardown.
			{
				"label": "Docker: Start Stack",
				"detail": "Build and start each selected checkout's stack sequentially, then wait for healthchecks. The default build task (Ctrl+Shift+B). A checkout with no Docker tier says so by name.",
				"type": "process",
				"command": "python",
				"args": [
					"${workspaceFolder:devkit}/scripts/devkit_project.py",
					"--project",
					"${input:project}",
					"docker-up"
				],
				"group": {
					"kind": "build",
					"isDefault": true
				},
				"icon": {
					"id": "vm-running",
					"color": "terminal.ansiGreen"
				},
				"presentation": {
					"reveal": "always",
					"panel": "new",
					"showReuseMessage": false,
					"close": false
				},
				"problemMatcher": []
			},
			{
				"label": "Docker: Stop Stack",
				"detail": "Stop each selected checkout's containers sequentially. Named volumes and their data survive — this is `down`, never `down -v`.",
				"type": "process",
				"command": "python",
				"args": [
					"${workspaceFolder:devkit}/scripts/devkit_project.py",
					"--project",
					"${input:project}",
					"docker-down"
				],
				"icon": {
					"id": "vm-outline",
					"color": "terminal.ansiYellow"
				},
				"presentation": {
					"reveal": "always",
					"panel": "new",
					"showReuseMessage": false,
					"close": false
				},
				"problemMatcher": []
			},
			{
				"label": "Docker: Restart Engine",
				"detail": "Unsticks a daemon hung on 'Starting the Docker Engine': kill processes → wsl --shutdown → relaunch → poll until `docker info` answers. Uses the chosen project's own scripts/docker-restart-engine.py when it ships one.",
				"type": "process",
				"command": "python",
				"args": [
					"${workspaceFolder:devkit}/scripts/devkit_project.py",
					"--project",
					"${input:daemonProject}",
					"docker-restart-engine"
				],
				"icon": {
					"id": "debug-restart",
					"color": "terminal.ansiBlue"
				},
				"presentation": {
					"reveal": "always",
					"panel": "new",
					"close": false,
					"clear": true
				},
				"problemMatcher": []
			},
			{
				"label": "Docker: Fix Stalled Desktop",
				"detail": "The aggressive escalation from 'Restart Engine' — two stop/start rounds and a doubled poll window, for a Docker Desktop that never finishes starting. Uses the chosen project's own scripts/docker-fix.py when it ships one.",
				"type": "process",
				"command": "python",
				"args": [
					"${workspaceFolder:devkit}/scripts/devkit_project.py",
					"--project",
					"${input:daemonProject}",
					"docker-fix"
				],
				"icon": {
					"id": "tools",
					"color": "terminal.ansiYellow"
				},
				"presentation": {
					"reveal": "always",
					"panel": "new",
					"close": false,
					"clear": true
				},
				"problemMatcher": []
			},
			{
				"label": "Docker: Prune + Compact VHDX",
				"detail": "For each selected checkout sequentially: reclaim image/build-cache space and compact the WSL2 VHDX. Never touches named volumes; project overrides may tear down and restore their own stack.",
				"type": "process",
				"command": "python",
				"args": [
					"${workspaceFolder:devkit}/scripts/devkit_project.py",
					"--project",
					"${input:project}",
					"docker-prune"
				],
				"icon": {
					"id": "trash",
					"color": "terminal.ansiRed"
				},
				"presentation": {
					"reveal": "always",
					"panel": "new",
					"close": false,
					"clear": true
				},
				"problemMatcher": []
			},
			{
				// Machine scope, not project scope — so no `${input:project}`. The three
				// things that make this laptop slow after a few hours are unrelated to
				// each other and only one of them is Docker's, which is why this is not
				// another mode of "Docker: Prune + Compact VHDX". See ACTIONS["reclaim"].
				"label": "Machine: Reclaim Resources",
				"detail": "Stops every running container (docker stop, never down — named volumes and stopped state survive), deletes RDP traces / WSL crash dumps / pytest scratch / node cache and loose %TEMP% files older than 3 days, then reconciles ephemeral boxes — but only once the disk is clear of the 20 GB floor reconcile reaps open PRs at. Reports commit charge; it cannot free memory.",
				"type": "process",
				"command": "python",
				"args": [
					"${workspaceFolder:devkit}/scripts/devkit_project.py",
					"--project",
					"devkit",
					"reclaim"
				],
				"icon": {
					"id": "flame",
					"color": "terminal.ansiBrightRed"
				},
				"presentation": {
					"reveal": "always",
					"panel": "new",
					"clear": true,
					"close": false
				},
				"problemMatcher": []
			},
			{
				// Reviewing a UI change before its PR merges, and the whole task is a Vite dev
				// server: `npm run dev` on each ticked ref's frontend, one free port each. NO
				// Docker — no box, no compose stack, no image build, no named volume.
				//
				// There were two preview tasks under this heading until 2026-08-25, and the
				// expensive one carried this label. It answered the same question with a
				// compose stack per branch (about three minutes cold), while the cheap one that
				// is all a UI review needs sat below it named `Serve UI Branches on Host` —
				// which nobody reads as "open a UI branch". Clicking the obvious label
				// therefore brought a stack up every time. There is one task now, it is the
				// cheap one, and `worktree.py preview` remains the CLI verb for the full-stack
				// kind: available to type, impossible to click by accident.
				//
				// Machine scope, not project scope — one box registry and one port registry on
				// this machine, so the checkout is a dimension inside the ANSWER rather than a
				// scope for the task: `previewProject` asks which one, `previewRow` lists that
				// one's refs. See ACTIONS["preview-ui-host"].
				//
				// `focus` because the terminal menu is still reachable — a pick that no longer
				// matches a row lands there — and without it that answer is typed into
				// whatever had focus when the task was clicked.
				"label": "Preview: Open a UI Branch",
				"detail": "Asks which checkout, then which of its refs — its default branch first, then live agent boxes, open PRs and recent agent/ branches on origin — and serves each ticked one's frontend from a plain host Vite dev server on its own port, opening the URL once it answers. No Docker and no backend: seconds to start, after a one-off npm install per new ref (about a minute). A ref with a live agent box is served from that box's worktree, unpushed work included; anything else gets a kept copy under .ui-previews/. API-backed views show real data when the static checkout's stack happens to be up and their offline state when it is not. The lists track GitHub on their own — the scheduled reconcile pass rebuilds them every quarter hour, so a PR that opened shows up and one that merged drops off. The servers end when this terminal does, by three separate means — Ctrl+C, a job object the kernel enforces, and a watch on the terminal itself — and 'Preview: Stop Host UI Servers' ends any that outlived all three.",
				"type": "process",
				"command": "python",
				"args": [
					"${workspaceFolder:devkit}/scripts/devkit_project.py",
					"--project",
					"devkit",
					"preview-ui-host",
					"--picks",
					"${input:previewRow}"
				],
				"icon": {
					"id": "preview",
					"color": "terminal.ansiBrightYellow"
				},
				"presentation": {
					"reveal": "always",
					"panel": "new",
					"focus": true,
					"clear": true,
					"close": false
				},
				// Same reason as its siblings — though one run already serves several refs,
				// a second batch later in the day should not offer to kill the first. Ports
				// are free-scanned per server, so instances cannot collide.
				"runOptions": {
					"instanceLimit": 4
				},
				"problemMatcher": []
			},
			{
				// The way out when a host preview outlived its terminal. It should never be
				// needed — the script's job object and its owner watch both end the servers
				// without anyone clicking anything — and it exists anyway because for months
				// nothing did, and three Vite servers sat on ports 5300–5303 for a whole
				// afternoon with no way to find them short of Task Manager. A guarantee you
				// cannot check by hand is one you end up trusting on faith.
				//
				// No picker and no `focus`: it asks nothing, prints what it stopped, and is
				// over in under a second. See ACTIONS["preview-ui-stop"].
				"label": "Preview: Stop Host UI Servers",
				"detail": "Stops every host Vite preview server running on this machine, whichever terminal started it, and says which ones it stopped. Nothing to stop is the normal answer — closing a preview's terminal already ends its servers — so this is for the one that got away. Docker previews are untouched: 'Machine: Reclaim Resources' is the verb for those.",
				"type": "process",
				"command": "python",
				"args": [
					"${workspaceFolder:devkit}/scripts/devkit_project.py",
					"--project",
					"devkit",
					"preview-ui-stop"
				],
				"icon": {
					"id": "debug-stop",
					"color": "terminal.ansiBrightRed"
				},
				"presentation": {
					"reveal": "always",
					"panel": "new",
					"clear": true,
					"close": false
				},
				"problemMatcher": []
			},
			// `Preview: Restart Standing Previews` stood here until 2026-08-25. It brought
			// every standing preview BOX back up after a reboot — a verb with nothing left
			// to act on once the clickable preview stopped cutting boxes, and its second job
			// (writing the dropdown's option file on a machine where nothing had yet) now
			// belongs to `worktree.py reconcile`, which does it every fifteen minutes on a
			// schedule. `worktree.py preview` is still there for a full-stack preview, and
			// `--all` still restarts whatever one of those left standing.
			// --- scoped: carameli's worktree pair --------------------------------
			// These came up out of `carameli/.vscode/tasks.json`. The picker lists two
			// checkouts rather than five because that is what the action is defined for —
			// see `Action.projects` in devkit_project.py.
			//
			// Three of them were test runners — the free-target picker, Playwright and the
			// local integration suite — and they are rows on "Test: Run Suite"'s kind menu
			// now. Their scope did not move with them: `Action.projects` still refuses a
			// checkout that cannot run them, and the menu says which checkouts each row is
			// for rather than hiding rows it cannot know the audience of.
			{
				"label": "Assets: Encode Comic-Book Art",
				"detail": "Encode every master in frontend/assets-src/comic-book/ that has no .webp in public/comic-book/ yet, and add each one to the ?edit=1 picture dropdown. Existing exports are skipped, so it is safe to repeat. Reports public/ against MAX_PUBLIC_BYTES and never raises it.",
				"type": "process",
				"command": "python",
				"args": [
					"${workspaceFolder:devkit}/scripts/devkit_project.py",
					"--project",
					"${input:carameliCheckout}",
					"encode-art"
				],
				"icon": {
					"id": "file-media",
					"color": "terminal.ansiBrightMagenta"
				},
				"presentation": {
					"reveal": "always",
					"panel": "new",
					"showReuseMessage": false,
					"close": false
				},
				"problemMatcher": []
			},
			{
				// This one was deleted from carameli's task file for being a one-line
				// wrapper around a documented script, under the old rule that a
				// PROJECT-level task had to justify its copy. That rule is gone with the
				// files it governed: there is no copy here to drift, so a one-click
				// tunnel-plus-resync costs nothing and the script stays the interface.
				"label": "Start: ngrok + Sync Webhook URLs",
				"detail": "Sequentially open an ngrok tunnel for each selected Carameli checkout, update its webhook environment values, and RECREATE app + worker. This interrupts in-flight work and changes each tunnel URL.",
				"type": "process",
				"command": "python",
				"args": [
					"${workspaceFolder:devkit}/scripts/devkit_project.py",
					"--project",
					"${input:carameliCheckout}",
					"ngrok"
				],
				"icon": {
					"id": "radio-tower",
					"color": "terminal.ansiBrightMagenta"
				},
				"presentation": {
					"reveal": "always",
					"panel": "new",
					"showReuseMessage": false,
					"close": false
				},
				"problemMatcher": []
			},
			// --- scoped: ibkr_trader's worktree pair -----------------------------
			// The five that came up out of `ibkr_trader/.vscode/tasks.json`. Each routes
			// through a `scripts/*.py` in that repo, which is what makes them dispatchable
			// at all — the two Backtest tasks named `.venv\Scripts\ibkr-trader.exe`
			// directly and needed `scripts/backtest-task.py` written before they could
			// move. Note there is no "Docker: Start Stack" story here: that project keeps
			// containers OFF by default, and the generic task says so by name.
			{
				"label": "IBKR: Open Gateway VNC Viewer",
				"detail": "Open one TigerVNC viewer per selected IBKR Trader checkout, sequentially. Each checkout needs its `ibkr` profile up; this task only opens viewers.",
				"type": "process",
				"command": "python",
				"args": [
					"${workspaceFolder:devkit}/scripts/devkit_project.py",
					"--project",
					"${input:ibkrCheckout}",
					"vnc",
					"${env:ProgramFiles}\\TigerVNC\\vncviewer.exe"
				],
				"icon": {
					"id": "device-desktop",
					"color": "terminal.ansiBrightBlue"
				},
				"presentation": {
					"reveal": "always",
					"panel": "new",
					"showReuseMessage": false,
					"close": false
				},
				"problemMatcher": []
			},
			{
				"label": "Ingest: Run Source",
				"detail": "Run the selected source pull sequentially in each selected IBKR Trader checkout. Needs each `db` container up; output goes to that checkout's artifacts/tasks/ingest-*.log.",
				"type": "process",
				"command": "python",
				"args": [
					"${workspaceFolder:devkit}/scripts/devkit_project.py",
